the Battle of Winterfell which took  place during season 8 episode 3 the long  night was the most epic battle on Game  of Thrones yet the production was to say  the least a brutal labor of love here  are just a few of the most amazing  things about it and how they were  brought to life warning spoilers ahead  over the course of 11 weeks a cast and  crew of 750 people worked in the worst  imaginable weather conditions battling  freezing temperatures and all the  elements to make history  director Miguel sapota Nick who has  directed some of the most legendary  Thrones episodes to date took the helm  here in order to keep off the undead  army a giant moat was created around  Winterfell the whites famously do not  like fire so to keep them away  Melisandre lights it all on fire yes the  fire you saw was real in order to  achieve this effect they used about 900  steel logs they chose steel instead of  wood as they would appear to catch fire  without completely burning away to make  the fire spread in the first place they  filled water troughs with gallons and  gallons of propane the fire was  important to add light to a battle that  was mostly shot in the dark of night a  fact that has been controversial for  Game of Thrones fans as it made it hard  to know what was happening at times  cinematographer Fabian Wagner told Wired  the showrunners decided that this had to  be a dark episode and everything they  wanted the audience to see is there a  lot of dead bodies pile up in this  episode and the crew used practical  effects whenever they could  many of the whites that pop up in the  crypt were heavily inspired by the look  of ancient mummies - Kang's skin and all  the whites were mostly a combination of  prosthetics and CGI prop dead bodies  meanwhile are not easy to handle they're  expensive heavy and hard to move around  the prop makers came up with a trick  following the Battle of the bastards  instead of having to stick individual  bodies up  they would stack up a mound of dummies  and create a detailed mold of them the  team created around 300 of these body  piles this episode wasn't going to end  without the death of a fan-favorite and  the long night gave us the demise of  Lyanna Mormont played by Bella Ramsey  who sacrifices herself to kill off the  giant white achieving this involved  complicated and groundbreaking CGI they  shot a piece of the scene on location in  order to get the backdrop while on set a  crew member held a giant head on a tall  pole while this was happening the camera  operators were using in cam this  technology allowed them to playback  animation of what the Giants performance  would look like that was then synced to  the camera this allowed the camera  operators to actually see the Giant at  his proper scale while on set then  against a green screen actor Ian white  who's over seven feet tall actually  played the giant he was filmed in a way  that made him seem like he was double  his size  Ramsey wasn't actually on set with him  so for the shots where the giant picks  her up he instead lifted up a green doll  covered in tracking markers that scene  was then motion captured in the final  layer for close-up shots Ramsey was  lifted up by a giant robotic arm that  picked her up and moved her exactly as  the giant would the giant wasn't even  close to the biggest creature involved  in the battle they were the three  dragons  Ragle drogon and Viserion famously  whenever you see shots of John and  Daenerys riding the dragons in reality  they're actually riding on what looks  like a big mechanical bull they also  actually shoot fire on set the rest of  what you see on screen as well as the  color of vissarion's flames is largely a  result of CGI animating Vissarion  breathing fire differed from the two  alive dragons part of that was because  Viserion breathes blue fire but also  this dragon is injured he's leaking as  VFX supervisor Joe Bower points out he  has a hole in his neck  he's missing half his face by the time  John snow comes face to face with him  you can see fire coming out of different  parts of his face the damage caused by  the dragon's fire was added by  production designers they walked around  with flamethrowers and covered rubble  with dirt and mud after 20 minutes of  watching a battle you're over it's a  poached nick said to Entertainment  Weekly so how do you stop it from being  a battle in that sense the biggest  inspiration for this battle was the  Battle of Helm's Deep from the Lord of  the Rings the two towers which sapota  Nick notes is actually three different  intercut battles but that doesn't mean  the action wasn't incredibly important  the star of the episode might have been  Maisie Williams who's Arya Stark  delivers the fatal blow to the night  king that saves Winterfell it was a long  road for Williams leading to this  episode she said the director called her  a gear before the long night start  shooting and told her to start training  but she had no idea what exactly she was  training for to prepare for some of the  sets tight spaces weapons master Tommy  Dunn gave Arya staff that would  disassemble allowing her to fight in  narrow spaces with the exception of some  of the more dangerous moments Williams  does most of her own stunts  in fact she's so committed to Arya that  the right-handed Williams learned how to  fight left-handed just so she could  fight the same way Arya did in the books  when she took down the night king she  was attached to a wire and launched into  him that sequence was mostly shot in  front of a green screen so the crew  could have better control over the  environment
 

